{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"As n grows, the probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to 0. The finite obstruction structures are exactly the locally stable mixed configurations in which the crossing edges form a matching: every vertex has at most one neighbor of the opposite color, so no vertex can flip. The prevalence of reachable obstructions scales like exp(Θ(n)): the expected number of final matchings of size αn is exp(n f(α)) with f(α)>0 for α in (0,1/2), while the consensus basin has probability exp(-Θ(n)).","counterconditions":["If the initial coloring is biased so that one color has density above the threshold for 2-core percolation, consensus may occur with high probability.","If the graph is bipartite or has no short cycles, the final matching size may be o(n) and the limit may change.","If updates are synchronous or if vertices are chosen adversarially, the process may not be cut-decreasing and the conclusion fails."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Let H be the subgraph of crossing edges. A vertex is unstable exactly when its H-degree is 2 or 3. Flipping such a vertex toggles all three incident edges, reducing total cut size by 1 (degree 2) or 3 (degree 3). The asynchronous process is therefore a cut-decreasing random walk that stops precisely when H has maximum degree 1, i.e. is a matching. On a random 3-regular graph the initial H is a Bernoulli(1/2) subgraph with a giant 2-core; the random flip cascade removes cycles but leaves a linear-size matching, so the final state is a nonempty stable mixed configuration with high probability.","optional_refs":["bedb799f1c89f932a83a5b104c1df0671fe5485f05dd25fcf0ba6245e561b0c7"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"As n grows, the probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1. The finite obstruction structures are locally stable mixed configurations 